text:
The diff tightens `parseRegExp` from `\d+` to `\d`, so the integer part of the number may only be a single digit. Any input like `'10kb'`, `'100mb'`, or `'12.5kb'` no longer matches the regex, so `parse()` falls through to `parseInt(val, 10)` and silently drops the unit multiplier — e.g. `bytes('10kb')` returns `10` instead of `10240`. Single-digit inputs like `'1kb'` still work (and that's all the existing suite checks, which is why it stays green), so this is an observable behavioural regression, not a refactor.

```json
{"defect": true, "testFile": {"path": "test/proof-test.js", "content": "var assert = require('assert');
var bytes = require('..');

describe('proof', function () {
  it('fails on the patched build', function () {
    assert.strictEqual(bytes.parse('10kb'), 10240);
    assert.strictEqual(bytes.parse('100mb'), 104857600);
    assert.strictEqual(bytes.parse('12.5kb'), 12800);
    assert.strictEqual(bytes.parse('-10kb'), -10240);
  });
});
"}, "note": "Changing \d+ to \d in parseRegExp restricts the integer part to one digit, so inputs like '10kb' or '100mb' no longer match and fall back to parseInt, losing the unit multiplier (bytes('10kb') returns 10 instead of 10240)."}
```

